{
 "poet": "Henry Wadsworth Longfellow",
 "system": "M",
 "lam": 1.0,
 "scheme": "none",
 "seed": 12,
 "model": "lora",
 "stop": "20 lines",
 "poem": "My mother is here, and again\nI pass the same path in silence;\nNo sound from her footsteps on nothing\nBut a faint and distant voice alone;\nNot a word of joy or of anguish,\nNought that I could hear but sadness.\nLook to the end; for everywhere\nThe same approach ever appears;\nA sound like the tread alone,\nAnd I believe she will arrive.\nWherefore have I left this retreat?\nWhy did I not tell you again?\nDo you know my grief?  What is happened?\nHow does my heart ache with anguish?\nI am not sure now who may probably\nMay be there by the darkness sitting.\nHow can it be so sadly?\nWhat has come into my life's problems?\nWhy should we think of dying?\nI pray God, I ask Him kindly, pray",
